'''attach''' (a̤-tach′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''attachen'', ''atachen'' (only in the legal sense, the lit. sense being of mod. adoption), < OF. ''atacher'', ''atachier'', later and mod. F. ''attacher'' (also without assibilation OF. ''ataquer'', mod. F. ''attaquer'', > E. ''attack'', q. v.) (= Pr. ''attacar'' = Sp. Pg. ''atacar'' = It. ''attaccare'': see ''attacca''), fasten, join, lit. tack to, < ''a''- (< L. ''ad'', to) + *''tac'' (not found in OF.), Genevese ''tache'' = Sp. Pg. ''tacha'' = It. ''tacca'', < Bret. ''tach'', a nail, = Ir. ''taca'', a nail, peg, = Gael. ''tacaid'', a nail, tack, etc.: see ''tack''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''detach''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' In ''law'', to take by legal authority. {{smaller|(''a'') To take bodily; arrest in person: now applied only to arrest of a person by civil process to answer for a contempt of court or disregard of its mandate, but formerly to arrests of all kinds: with ''for'', also formerly with ''of''.}}
{{smaller block|There were two or three ''attached for'' the same robbery.
{{right|''Latimer'', 4th Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}
''Of'' capital treason I ''attach'' you both.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 2.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') To take (real or personal property) by legal warrant, to be held for the satisfaction of the judgment that may be rendered in a suit. See ''attachment''.}}
'''2†.''' To lay hold of; seize.
{{smaller block|<poem>Then, homeward, every man ''attach'' the hand
Of his fair mistress. {{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., iv. 3.}}</poem>}}
'''3.''' To take, seize, or lay hold on, by moral force, as by affection or interest; fasten or bind by moral influence; win: as, his kindness ''attached'' us all to him.
{{smaller block|<poem>Songs, garlands, flowers,
And charming symphonies ''attach’d'' the heart
Of Adam. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 595.}}</poem>}}

