¶
{{smaller block|The next group consists of those Rotifera which seldom or never ''attach'' themselves by the foot, but swim freely through the water.
{{right|''W. B. Carpenter'', Micros., § 453.}}}}
{{smaller block|Such temperaments . . . ''attach'' themselves, like barnacles, to what seems permanent.
{{right|''Lowell'', Fireside Travels, p. 67.}}}}

'''5.''' Figuratively, to connect; associate: as, to ''attach'' a particular significance to a word.
{{smaller block|He ''attaches'' very little importance to the invention of gunpowder.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Machiavelli.}}}}

'''6.''' To join to or with in action or function; connect as an associate or adjunct; adjoin for duty or companionship: as, an officer is ''attached'' to such a ship, regiment, battalion, etc.; our regiment is ''attached'' to the 1st brigade; this man is ''attached'' to my service; he ''attached'' himself to me for the entire journey.—{{smaller|'''Attached column''', in ''arch.'', same as ''engaged column'' (which see, under ''column'').}} ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|To seize, distrain, distress.—'''3.''' To win, gain over, engage, charm, endear one’s self to, captivate.—'''4.''' ''Add'', ''Affix'', ''Annex'', etc. See ''add''.—'''5.''' To attribute.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To adhere; pertain, as a quality or circumstance; belong or be incident: with ''to''.
{{smaller block|The fame of each discovery rightly ''attaches to'' the mind that made the formula which contains all the details, and not to the manufacturers who now make their gain by it.
{{right|''Emerson'', Success.}}}}
{{smaller block|''To'' the healthful performance of each function of mind or body ''attaches'' a pleasurable feeling.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 92.}}}}

'''2.''' To be fixed or fastened; rest as an appurtenance: with ''on'' or ''upon''.
{{smaller block|Blame ''attached upon'' Lord Aberdeen’s Cabinet for yielding.
{{right|''Kinglake'', Crimea, I. 491. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

