{{right|''Brougham.''}}}}
'''3.''' To make an onset or attempt upon, in a general sense; begin action upon or in regard to; set about or upon: as, to ''attack'' a piece of work or a problem, or (humorously) the dinner.—'''4.''' To begin to affect; come or fall upon; seize: said of diseases and other destructive agencies: as, yesterday he was ''attacked'' by fever; caries ''attacked'' the bones; locusts ''attacked'' the crops. Specifically—'''5.''' In ''chem.'', to cause to decompose or dissolve.
{{smaller block|The bodies are of a siliceous character, for they are not destroyed by ignition, nor ''attacked'' by hydrochloric acid.
{{right|''Science'', VII. 218.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' ''Set upon, Fall upon'', etc. (see ''assail''), assault, beset, besiege, beleaguer, charge upon, engage, challenge, combat.—'''2.''' To impugn, criticize, censure.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To make an attack or onset: as, the enemy ''attacked'' with great boldness.
{{smaller block|Those that ''attack'' generally get the victory.
{{right|''Cane'', Campaigns.}}}}

¶
'''attack''' (a̤-tak′), ''n.'' [= F. ''attaque''; from the verb.] '''1.''' A falling on with force or violence, or with calumny, satire, or criticism; an onset; an assault.
{{smaller block|I wish that he [Mr. Sumner] may know the shudder of terror which ran through all this community on the first tidings of this brutal ''attack.''
{{right|''Emerson'', Assault upon Mr. Sumner.}}}}
'''2.''' Battle generally; fight. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Long time in even scale
The battel hung; till Satan, . . .
. . . ranging through the dire ''attack'', . . .
Saw where the sword of Michael smote, and fell’d</poem>}}

